---
intent: >
  Refactor buildBrainState to the four-state vocabulary contract: drop the
  `invalid-path` variant entirely, remove all `stat()` work, shrink the
  remaining variants to `name` + `hints` (no `rootPath`/`connector`/`entryFile`/
  `tools`), and keep the structural drift comparison against the host's
  `.mcp.json` brain entry. Detector becomes a two-read pipeline (parse
  brain.aide → read .mcp.json → compare) returning `ok | no-brain-aide |
  no-mcp-entry | mcp-drift`.
---

## Project Structure

The detector remains a single-file orchestrator at this scope. Helper
subfolders are not warranted — the new pipeline has fewer steps than the
prior one (one fewer read, no stat) and every step is a 5–10 line
operation. If a single step's logic ever crosses the ~30-line threshold
named in the spec's Strategy (e.g. drift comparison gains a structurally
complex partial-equality rule), factor that step into a sibling subfolder
at that point.

```
src/service/buildBrainState/
├── .aide                ← [existing, aligned] intent spec — read first
├── plan.aide            ← [this file]
├── todo.aide            ← [existing, aligned] all spec-alignment items checked
├── index.ts             ← [rewrite] orchestrator implementing the new pipeline
│                           default export signature unchanged:
│                             default async function buildBrainState(
│                               root: string
│                             ): Promise<BrainState>
└── index.test.ts        ← [rewrite] co-located tests covering all four states
                            plus boundary cases; uses real temp dirs
```

The default export stays a single positional `(root: string)` per the
spec's single-input rule. JSDoc on the export summarizes all four states.
Tier 3 inline comments narrate each pipeline step inside the orchestrator.

## Prerequisites

This plan sits in the middle of the brain-layer dependency chain laid out
in `.aide/handoff.aide`. Two upstream modules must complete their refactors
first; this plan's contract names exactly what it consumes from each.

**`src/types/index.ts`** — types are upstream of every consumer. Before this
plan runs, the central types module must export:

- `BrainAideConfig` collapsed to `{ name: string; mcpServerConfig: { command:
  string; args: string[] } }`. No `connector`, no `rootPath`, no
  `entryFile`, no `tools`.
- `ParseBrainAideResult` unchanged in shape (the four-variant tagged union:
  `ok | missing | malformed-frontmatter | malformed-body`); the `config`
  payload on the `ok` variant carries the new `BrainAideConfig`.
- `BrainState` REPLACED with the new four-variant union (this plan owns
  the redefinition — see Step 1). The old five-variant shape with
  `rootPath` / `connector` / `invalid-path` is gone.
- `BrainToolResult` retains `{ status: BrainState["status"]; instructions:
  string }`; the status union widens to the new four values automatically.

**`src/service/parseBrainAide/`** — the parser must accept the new
two-field schema before this detector can consume its `ok` payload. The
contract this plan depends on:

- `parseBrainAide(root: string): Promise<ParseBrainAideResult>` — default
  export. On `ok`, the returned `config` matches the new `BrainAideConfig`
  (only `name` and `mcpServerConfig`). On any malformed-frontmatter case
  (including the parser actively rejecting deprecated fields like
  `rootPath`), the result is `{ kind: "malformed-frontmatter", reason }`.
  Never throws.
- `interpolateArgs(config: BrainAideConfig): string[]` — named export. Returns
  a copy of `config.mcpServerConfig.args` with every `${<key>}` substituted
  against the matching top-level frontmatter field. With the schema
  collapsed to `name` + `mcpServerConfig`, `${name}` is the only resolvable
  placeholder; the default scaffold contains no placeholders, so the call
  is a no-op for default installs. The detector calls this unconditionally
  per the spec's "always interpolate, then compare" contract.

If either upstream module has not landed when this plan starts, halt and
escalate — this plan cannot be partially executed against the old
`BrainAideConfig` shape.

## Plan

### 1. Replace the `BrainState` union in `src/types/index.ts`

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/type-system`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [x] Replace the existing five-variant `BrainState` definition with the
      new four-variant tagged union. The new shape, per the spec's
      `outcomes.desired[12]` and Good examples:

      ```
      type BrainState =
        | { status: "ok"; name: string; hints: BrainHint[] }
        | { status: "no-brain-aide"; hints: BrainHint[] }
        | { status: "no-mcp-entry"; name: string; hints: BrainHint[] }
        | { status: "mcp-drift"; name: string; hints: BrainHint[] }
      ```

      Discriminant invariants (encode in JSDoc on the union and on each
      variant's `name`/`hints` fields):

      - `ok` carries the user-declared `name` label from brain.aide
        (descriptive only, never dispatched on by any code) and `hints`.
        No `rootPath`, no `connector`, no `entryFile`, no `tools`, no
        `backend` — all five fields are gone from the contract.
      - `no-brain-aide` carries only `hints`. No `name` — the file is
        unusable (missing or malformed) and there is nothing to read a
        label from.
      - `no-mcp-entry` carries `name` (from the parsed brain.aide) and
        `hints`. The orchestrator names the wired brand in remediation
        prose.
      - `mcp-drift` carries `name` and `hints`. The user's brain.aide
        and `.mcp.json` disagree on `command` or `args`; remediation
        is `npx aidemd-mcp sync`.
      - `hints` is populated unconditionally on every variant.
      - The retired `invalid-path` variant must not appear under that
        name or any rename. Path validity is the launcher's problem.

      Drop the `import type { BackendDriver }` if it still exists; no
      variant carries a backend driver. Remove every JSDoc reference to
      `rootPath`, `connector`, `entryFile`, `tools`, `backend`, or
      `invalid-path` from the `BrainState` block.

      Verify `BrainToolResult` still reads `{ status: BrainState["status"];
      instructions: string }` — the status union widens automatically to
      the new four values. No `backend` field on `BrainToolResult` either.

      Verify `InfoResult.brain: BrainState` — the field's type widens
      automatically. No edit beyond the `BrainState` redefinition.

      Run `npx tsc --noEmit` after the edit; expect compile errors in
      consumer files (`src/tools/info`, `src/tools/brain`,
      `src/service/buildBrainState`). Those are dependency-contract
      failures owned by the consumers' own plans (this plan resolves the
      `buildBrainState` errors in Steps 2 and 3; the `tools/*` errors are
      addressed by their respective plans).

### 2. Rewrite the detector orchestrator in `src/service/buildBrainState/index.ts`

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/data-flow`, `coding-playbook/services/error-handling`

- [x] Rewrite `src/service/buildBrainState/index.ts` to implement the new
      four-state pipeline. The default export signature is unchanged:
      `default async function buildBrainState(root: string):
      Promise<BrainState>`.

      Drop the `stat` import from `node:fs/promises` — the detector no
      longer stats any path. Keep `readFile` for `.mcp.json`. Keep the
      imports of `parseBrainAide`, `interpolateArgs`, `detectFramework`,
      `resolveBrainHints`. Drop any retired-backend-registry imports if
      any remain.

      The orchestrator pipeline (Tier 3 inline-narrated, top-to-bottom):

      1. Resolve framework config via `detectFramework(root)`. Compute
         `mcpConfigPath = join(root, frameworkConfig.mcpConfigPath)`.
         Unchanged from the prior pipeline.
      2. Resolve hints via `resolveBrainHints(root)`. Populate
         unconditionally so `hints` appears on every returned state
         regardless of which branch fires.
      3. Call `parseBrainAide(root)`. Branch on `result.kind`:
         - `"missing"` → return `{ status: "no-brain-aide", hints }`.
         - `"malformed-frontmatter"` → return `{ status: "no-brain-aide",
           hints }`. (All malformed-frontmatter sub-cases — including
           the parser's rejection of deprecated fields — collapse to
           the same status; the file is unusable until the user fixes
           it. The granular `reason` lives on the parser's result and
           is consumed by other surfaces, not by this detector.)
         - `"malformed-body"` → return `{ status: "no-brain-aide",
           hints }`. Same rationale.
         - `"ok"` → continue with `const { config } = result;`.
      4. Read `.mcp.json` at `mcpConfigPath` via `readFile`. Wrap the
         call in try/catch. On ENOENT or any I/O failure, return
         `{ status: "no-mcp-entry", name: config.name, hints }`. Threading
         the parsed `name` through is required by the new shape so the
         orchestrator can name the wired brand in remediation prose.
      5. Parse the `.mcp.json` contents via `JSON.parse`. Wrap in
         try/catch. On parse failure, return `{ status: "no-mcp-entry",
         name: config.name, hints }`. Same remediation as I/O failure.
      6. Look up `mcpServers.brain` on the parsed object. Use the same
         defensive narrowing pattern as the prior implementation
         (object-shape checks + `"brain" in servers`); on any structural
         miss — `mcpServers` absent, `mcpServers` not an object, `brain`
         key absent, `brain` value not an object, `command` not a string,
         `args` not an array — return `{ status: "no-mcp-entry", name:
         config.name, hints }`. No fallback to a legacy `obsidian` key
         under any circumstance. Capture the actual `command` and `args`
         into local variables for the next step.
      7. Compute the expected entry from the parsed brain.aide:
         `expectedCommand = config.mcpServerConfig.command`
         `expectedArgs = interpolateArgs(config)`
         The `interpolateArgs` call is unconditional per the spec's
         "always interpolate, then compare" contract — in the default
         flow it returns args unchanged because no placeholders are
         present.
      8. Structural drift comparison:
         - `commandMatches = actualCommand === expectedCommand` (string
           equality)
         - `argsMatch = actualArgs.length === expectedArgs.length &&
           expectedArgs.every((expected, i) => actualArgs[i] === expected)`
           (length-then-element-by-element string equality)
         If either check fails, return `{ status: "mcp-drift", name:
         config.name, hints }`.
      9. Happy path — everything agrees → return `{ status: "ok", name:
         config.name, hints }`.

      Hard invariants (encode as orchestrator JSDoc plus a few inline
      comments where the rule is non-obvious):

      - The detector NEVER throws. Every failure mode collapses to a
        structured `BrainState` value.
      - The detector NEVER stats any path. No `stat`, no `existsSync`,
        no `lstat`, not even as a "courtesy" check on a string extracted
        from args.
      - The detector NEVER imports from any retired backend registry
        (`brainBackends/`, `resolveBackend`, `getDriverById`, etc.).
      - The detector NEVER branches on `config.name` or any value derived
        from it.
      - The detector NEVER auto-syncs `.mcp.json` on drift. No writes
        to `.mcp.json` from this module under any circumstance.
      - The detector NEVER falls back to a legacy `mcpServers.obsidian`
        key.
      - The detector NEVER introspects `mcpServerConfig.args` beyond
        element-by-element equality comparison.
      - Pipeline order is fixed: brain.aide first, then `.mcp.json`.
        Reading `.mcp.json` first would invert the source-of-truth
        direction.

### 3. Rewrite the co-located tests in `src/service/buildBrainState/index.test.ts`

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

