## Decisions

- **Four states, not five — `invalid-path` is gone.** The spec's
  `outcomes.desired[1]` and the parent intent's `outcomes.desired[10]`
  both retire the `invalid-path` state. With `rootPath` removed from
  the brain.aide schema there is no path the package owns to validate.
  Path validity becomes the launcher's problem at MCP server startup;
  reintroducing a stat — even a "courtesy" `existsSync` — re-creates
  the retired state and shifts a load-bearing contract surface back
  into the package.

- **Variants shrink to `name` + `hints`.** The `ok`, `no-mcp-entry`,
  and `mcp-drift` shapes carry only the user-declared `name` label and
  `hints`; `no-brain-aide` carries only `hints`. Every other field
  (`rootPath`, `connector`, `entryFile`, `tools`, `backend`) is gone.
  Consumers branch on `status` alone, and the wired brain is described
  by `name` (descriptive only) on the states that carry it. The
  brain's interface contract — which MCP tools to call — lives in the
  prose body the runtime brain tool returns verbatim, not in any
  frontmatter declaration the detector reports.

- **`name` is metadata, not dispatch.** The detector reads `name` from
  parsed brain.aide and threads it through unchanged. No code path in
  this detector branches on its value. This is the renamed enforcement
  of the prior `connector`-not-dispatched-on contract; the rule is
  unchanged, only the field name moves.

- **Malformed brain.aide collapses to `no-brain-aide`.** All three
  parser-failure variants (`missing`, `malformed-frontmatter`,
  `malformed-body`) — including the new case where the parser actively
  rejects deprecated fields — flow through the same status. The user's
  remediation is identical across all three ("fix or recreate the
  file"); a separate status per sub-case would force every consumer to
  learn three branches for one fix path. The granular `reason` lives
  on the parser's result for surfaces that want it.

- **Pipeline shrinks to two reads.** Old pipeline: parse brain.aide →
  stat rootPath → read `.mcp.json` → compare. New pipeline: parse
  brain.aide → read `.mcp.json` → compare. The stat step is gone with
  `rootPath`; the source-of-truth direction (brain.aide canonical,
  `.mcp.json` derived) is unchanged.

- **Drift is structural-equality on the in-memory entry shape.** Two
  args lists are equal if they have the same length and every position
  has the same string value; commands are compared with `===`. Object
  key order, whitespace, and trailing newlines in serialized
  `.mcp.json` are not part of the comparison because both sides are
  parsed in-memory before comparing. This avoids spurious drift on
  `.mcp.json` files the user reformatted.

- **`interpolateArgs` is called unconditionally.** The spec's
  `outcomes.desired[5]` makes this load-bearing: "the call is the
  contract." In the default scaffold (no placeholders) it returns args
  unchanged; advanced users who DRY a value via `${name}` rely on the
  call running. Skipping it when no placeholders are present would
  fork the code path for the advanced case.

- **Single-orchestrator file, no helper subfolders today.** The spec's
  Strategy paragraph names ~30 lines per step as the threshold for
  splitting. The new pipeline's largest step (the .mcp.json defensive
  narrowing into actualCommand/actualArgs) sits well below that
  threshold. If drift comparison ever grows structurally complex
  (e.g. a future schema adds optional fields requiring deep
  partial-equality), factor that step into a sibling subfolder at
  that point.

- **Tests use real temp dirs and real brain.aide / .mcp.json files,
  not fs mocks.** The detector's I/O surface is two `readFile` calls
  (one inside `parseBrainAide`, one for `.mcp.json`); mocking either
  would test the mock more than the detector. Temp dirs cost ~5ms per
  test and ensure the parser-driven failure modes match real Node
  semantics. The pattern matches the prior test file and the project's
  existing convention (see parseBrainAide's tests).

- **Plan scope is buildBrainState's module only.** Compile errors in
  consumers (`src/tools/info`, `src/tools/brain`, `src/cli/sync`)
  triggered by the `BrainState` type collapse are dependency-contract
  failures owned by those modules' own plans. This plan logs them in
  Step 4 but does not patch them — patching would conflate scopes and
  hide the dependency chain the handoff documents.
